yesterday President Trump said the  Paycheck protection program one of the  federal initiatives designed to help  small businesses stay afloat processed  more than three billion dollars worth of  loans but as business owners apply to  the programs some have run into a  disturbing technical glitch Jim Axelrod  has the story  Eric Levinson put everything he had and  is starting the hummingbird in the pet  friendly bed-and-breakfast he opened in  Maryland in 2017 things were good until  coronavirus forced Levinson to close his  doors to go from a steady cash inflow  that you know obviously ebbs and flows  during the year to completely flatlining  no new reservations no new bookings so  no income at all Levinson turned to the  Small Business Administration or the SBA  for a disaster loan he went online and  opened the registration page and found  someone else's personal information name  address everything should have been  blank  had someone else's information in it  already correct date of birth social  security number email phone numbers  business address all the basic  information he deleted that information  and typed in his own ten minutes later  Levinson got a call from a business in  Delaware that had all of his information  you go on the computer and there's  someone else's information in the  application and then you get a call from  someone you've never met before and your  information all right he's on her  application correct what a mess I know  so on top of the stress I have trying to  keep this business going now I don't  know who else could have seen that  information Levinson isn't alone nearly  a dozen other small businesses across  the country told CBS News they  experienced the same problem last week  this couldn't come at a worse time for  small businesses according to Todd  McCracken with the National Small  Business Association they've gotten  assistance is coming that they can can  on those horses and these kinds of  wishes happen it really undercuts their  confidence in the system and Mike needs  me to make different decisions they  would have otherwise the SBA's website  went through various shutdowns for  maintenance last week but the agency  told CBS News this site is currently  working that comforting to you on the  site sense it's not comforting to me I  have no choice  Levinson says he tried emailing tweeting  even attending an online SBA a webinar  to alert them to the problem he hasn't  heard anything back the SBA is yet to  even acknowledge the leaks say for how  long they had the problem or even the  number of businesses that may have been  impacted for CBS this morning Saturday  this is Jim Axelrod in Montclair New  Jersey
 

